// Module 3 — Async & Event-Driven: queues, logs, pub/sub, fan-out, CQRS, sagas and batch.
(function (T) {

  T({
    id: '3.1', mod: '3', title: 'Message queues', tag: 'Hand each job to one worker, safely',
    analogy: 'A ticket rail in a kitchen where a cook who takes a ticket has ten minutes to send the dish. When the dish goes out the ticket is spiked; if the ten minutes pass first, the ticket goes back on the rail for another cook. Nothing is lost when a cook walks out, and a slow cook can find someone else cooking the same dish.',
    points: [
      'A work queue gives each message to one consumer, and receiving it does not delete it. SQS hides a received message for the visibility timeout (default 30 s, maximum 12 hours); the consumer deletes it when the work is done, and if it does not, the message becomes visible again for another consumer. With manual acknowledgements, RabbitMQ requeues a delivery that was never acknowledged when the consumer\'s channel or connection closes; with automatic acknowledgements it is lost.',
      'So delivery is at least once. A crash between doing the work and deleting the message means the work is done twice, and SQS standard queues can deliver a duplicate even without a crash. Consumers dedupe on a message or business ID, as topic 0C.4 describes.',
      'Size the visibility timeout above the slowest normal job, and heartbeat (ChangeMessageVisibility) for long ones. Too short, and under load a job outlives its timeout, a second worker starts the same message and the extra work slows everything further; too long, and a crashed worker\'s message waits that long before it is retried.',
      'A poison message fails every time. Cap the attempts (SQS maxReceiveCount; RabbitMQ 4.0+ quorum queues default to a delivery limit of 20, then drop the message unless a dead-letter exchange is configured), move it to a dead-letter queue, alarm on anything arriving there, and redrive it after the fix.',
      'SQS standard queues give nearly unlimited throughput with best-effort ordering. FIFO queues keep strict order within a message group and drop duplicate sends inside a 5-minute window, but each group is processed one message at a time, and in default mode a FIFO queue takes 300 API calls/s per action without batching (3,000 messages/s in batches of 10). High throughput mode raises that limit, and AWS says its capacity grows with the number of message groups. Use many group IDs, such as one per customer.',
      'Size consumers from the backlog, not from CPU. AWS\'s rule: acceptable backlog per instance = acceptable latency ÷ processing time per message, so a 10 s target at 0.1 s a message allows 100 messages per instance. Alarm on the age of the oldest message, which shows how far behind the system really is.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Setting the visibility timeout below the slowest real job. The first time a dependency slows down, messages reappear while they are still being worked on, two or three workers process each one, and the queue overloads itself just when it has the least capacity to spare.',
    say: 'Workers delete a message only after the work commits, so handlers dedupe on the order ID. Jobs take about 2 s, so I use a 60 s visibility timeout with a heartbeat, dead-letter after 5 receives, and scale on backlog per worker: a 30 s target at 2 s a job is 15 messages per worker.',
    refs: [
      ['aws-sqs-visibility-timeout', 'Receiving hides, it does not delete — “When you receive a message from an Amazon SQS queue, it remains in the queue but becomes temporarily invisible to other consumers.”'],
      ['aws-sqs-visibility-timeout', 'Not deleted in time — “If you don\'t delete it before the timeout expires, the message becomes visible again in the queue and can be retrieved by another consumer.”'],
      ['aws-bl-queue-backlogs', 'A deadline shorter than the work — “If the code keeps running and forgets about this deadline, the same message can be delivered multiple times in parallel.”'],
      ['aws-sqs-queue-types', 'FIFO throughput — “Without batching, FIFO queues support up to 300 API calls per second per API method (SendMessage, ReceiveMessage, or DeleteMessage).”'],
      ['aws-sqs-fifo-key-terms', 'One message at a time per group — “Messages within the same message group are always processed one at a time, in strict order, ensuring that no two messages from the same group are processed simultaneously.”'],
      ['aws-sqs-high-throughput-fifo', 'Spread the groups — “AWS recommends that you use message group IDs that can have a large number of distinct values.”'],
      ['aws-sqs-dlq', 'Dead-letter queues break FIFO order — “Don\'t use a dead-letter queue with a FIFO queue if you don\'t want to break the exact order of messages or operations.”'],
      ['aws-sqs-high-throughput-fifo', 'High throughput needs many groups — “To enhance request capacity in high throughput FIFO queues, increasing the number of message groups is recommended.”'],
      ['rabbitmq-quorum-queues', 'A default retry cap in RabbitMQ — “Starting with RabbitMQ 4.0, the delivery limit for quorum queues defaults to 20.”'],
      ['rabbitmq-quorum-queues', 'At the limit — “When a message has been redelivered more times than the limit the message will be dropped (removed) or dead-lettered (if a DLX is configured).”'],
      ['rabbitmq-consumer-acks', 'Manual acknowledgement requeues — “When manual acknowledgements are used, any delivery (message) that was not acked is automatically requeued when the channel (or connection) on which the delivery happened is closed.”'],
      ['rabbitmq-consumer-acks', 'Automatic acknowledgement loses messages — “if consumers\'s TCP connection or channel is closed before successful delivery, the message sent by the server will be lost.”'],
      ['aws-sqs-message-quotas', 'Retention — “By default, a message is retained for 4 days.”'],
      ['aws-asg-sqs-scaling', 'Acceptable backlog per instance — “take the acceptable latency value and divide it by the average time that an EC2 instance takes to process a message.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['What happens to an SQS message between ReceiveMessage and DeleteMessage?', 'It stays in the queue but is invisible to other consumers for the visibility timeout (30 s by default, up to 12 hours). If the consumer deletes it in time it is gone; if not, it becomes visible again and another consumer can receive it.'],
      ['Why is a dead-letter queue awkward on a FIFO queue?', 'Moving a failed message aside lets the rest of its message group carry on, so the later messages are processed without it and the exact order is broken. AWS advises against a DLQ on FIFO queues where the order of operations matters.'],
      ['How do you work out how many consumers a queue needs?', 'Divide the acceptable latency by the processing time per message to get the acceptable backlog per consumer, then scale so that visible messages ÷ consumers stays at or below it. Alarm on the age of the oldest message as well.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['Workers each handle one message at a time, taking 0.2 s per message, and messages may wait at most 20 s. The queue holds 12,000 visible messages and 40 workers are running. Using AWS\'s backlog-per-instance method, how many workers do you need?', ['120 workers', '600 workers', '2,400 workers', '40 workers'], 'Acceptable backlog per worker = 20 s ÷ 0.2 s = 100 messages, and 12,000 ÷ 100 = 120. 600 is 12,000 ÷ 20, the message rate needed, read as a worker count; 2,400 is 12,000 × 0.2, the worker-seconds of work, never divided by the 20 s allowed; 40 leaves 300 messages per worker, a 60 s wait.', ['aws-asg-sqs-scaling']],
      ['Jobs on an SQS standard queue normally take 20 s and the visibility timeout is 30 s. A database slowdown stretches every job to 45 s. What happens to the load on the workers?', ['Each message reappears mid-job and a second worker starts it too', 'Each message moves to the dead-letter queue after its first timeout', 'SQS extends the timeout itself while the worker is still connected', 'Load stays flat, since each message stays hidden until it is deleted'], 'At 30 s the message becomes visible again while the first worker is still on it, so another worker starts the same job, and the duplicates add load to a system that is already slow. AWS describes this as an overloaded service fork-bombing itself, and heartbeats that extend the timeout as the fix. A message reaches the DLQ only after maxReceiveCount receives, not on its first timeout.', ['aws-bl-queue-backlogs', 'aws-sqs-visibility-timeout']],
      ['Price updates for 50,000 products must be applied in order per product, at a peak of 20,000 messages/s. A design puts them on one SQS FIFO queue with the same message group ID, "prices", on every message. What is wrong?', ['One group is processed a message at a time; use the product ID as the group', 'Nothing, provided high throughput mode is on, which allows up to 30,000 TPS', 'FIFO order breaks with many consumers; run one consumer for the whole queue', 'Nothing, provided enough consumers poll the queue in parallel with each other'], 'Messages in one group are processed one at a time in strict order, so a single group serialises the whole queue; more consumers or high throughput mode cannot change that, and a single consumer adds nothing, since SQS keeps each group in order whichever consumer receives it. Order is needed only per product, so the product ID as the group ID keeps that order and lets thousands of groups run in parallel, which is what AWS recommends.', ['aws-sqs-fifo-key-terms', 'aws-sqs-high-throughput-fifo']],
      ['An SQS FIFO queue carries edit operations, with the document ID as the message group ID. One malformed edit for document D fails on every attempt, and the queue has no dead-letter queue. What happens?', ['D\'s later edits wait behind it; other documents carry on', 'D\'s later edits go ahead, and the bad one retries on its own', 'The whole queue stops until someone deletes the bad message', 'SQS drops it once the 5-minute deduplication window ends'], 'While a message in a group is in flight, SQS holds back the rest of that group until it is deleted or its visibility timeout expires, and then the same message is delivered again. Other groups are unaffected. A DLQ would free D, but D\'s later edits would then apply without the failed one, which is why AWS warns against DLQs on FIFO queues where order matters. With no DLQ the block lasts until the message\'s retention period runs out (4 days by default); SQS then deletes it silently and D\'s later edits run without it. Deduplication applies to sends, not to failed processing.', ['aws-sqs-visibility-timeout', 'aws-sqs-dlq']],
      ['A RabbitMQ consumer is switched to automatic acknowledgement to raise throughput. A deploy restarts it while 500 deliveries sit in its in-memory buffer. What happens to those 500 messages?', ['They are lost: the broker counted them as done when it sent them', 'They are requeued once the consumer\'s connection has closed', 'They are dead-lettered, as the consumer did not acknowledge them', 'They are redelivered to it with the redeliver flag set to true'], 'In automatic mode a message counts as delivered the moment it is sent, so whatever the consumer was holding is gone. Requeueing on connection close, with the redeliver flag, is what manual acknowledgement buys. Automatic acks also remove the prefetch limit, so a slow consumer can buffer deliveries until it runs out of memory.', ['rabbitmq-consumer-acks']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how a work queue stops a job being lost when a worker dies, and what that asks of the consumer.', ['Receiving hides the message; delete or ack only after the work is done', 'At-least-once delivery, so consumers are idempotent', 'Visibility timeout above real job time, with a heartbeat for long jobs', 'A retry cap and a dead-letter queue for poison messages, with an alarm', 'Scale on backlog per consumer and age of oldest message; FIFO groups limit parallelism']],
    duck: ['If the worker already has the message, why does the queue still keep a copy?', 'Why not set the visibility timeout to a whole day so that nothing is ever processed twice?'],
    lab: { type: 'seq', parts: [
      { type: 'estimate', brief: 'Size a worker fleet for a queue.', fixed: [
        { q: 'Messages arrive at 600 per second. A worker runs 4 messages at once and each takes 0.5 s. How many workers keep them at or below 70% busy?', a: 108, unit: 'workers', tol: 0.05, how: 'One worker: 4 ÷ 0.5 s = 8 messages/s. 600 ÷ (8 × 0.7) = 107.1, so round up to 108.' },
        { q: 'Messages may wait at most 30 s, and a worker takes 0.25 s per message, one at a time. What backlog per worker should the scaling policy target?', a: 120, unit: 'messages', tol: 0, how: '30 ÷ 0.25 = 120. Scale out when visible messages ÷ workers rises above 120.' },
        { q: 'A FIFO queue has 200 active message groups. Each message takes 50 ms and each group is processed one message at a time. Ignoring API limits, what is the most it can process?', a: 4000, unit: 'messages/s', tol: 0.05, how: 'Each group: 1 ÷ 0.05 s = 20 messages/s. 200 groups × 20 = 4,000 messages/s. More groups, not more workers, raise this ceiling.' },
        { q: 'A message spends 1 day in an SQS standard queue, then moves to a dead-letter queue with 4 days of retention. After how many more days is it deleted from the DLQ?', a: 3, unit: 'days', tol: 0, how: 'For standard queues expiry keeps the original enqueue time: 4 − 1 = 3 days. Give the DLQ a longer retention than the source queue.' }
      ] },
      { type: 'sort', brief: 'Which mechanism deals with this?', buckets: ['Visibility timeout and heartbeat', 'Dead-letter queue', 'Idempotent consumer', 'FIFO message group'], items: [
        ['A worker crashes mid-job and the message must come back', 0], ['A job that legitimately runs for 10 minutes', 0], ['Stop a second worker taking a message being processed', 0],
        ['A payload that makes the handler throw on every attempt', 1], ['Keep failed messages to inspect and redrive after a fix', 1], ['An alarm that a deploy has started rejecting messages', 1],
        ['A standard queue hands out the same message twice', 2], ['Make a redelivered "charge order 42" message harmless', 2],
        ['Price updates for one product must apply in sequence', 3], ['Run 1,000 customers in parallel, each kept in order', 3]
      ] }
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '3.2', mod: '3', title: 'Stream processing', tag: 'An ordered log you can reread',
    analogy: 'A ship\'s logbook. Entries are only ever added at the bottom, each reader keeps a bookmark at the last line they read, and anyone can turn back the pages to check a figure again. The state of the ship is whatever the entries add up to.',
    points: [
      'Kafka stores a topic as partitions, each an append-only log. Events with the same key go to the same partition, and order is guaranteed only within a partition. A consumer\'s progress is one offset per partition, and events stay after they are read (for a set period, say a week), so a consumer can rewind and reread.',
      'A consumer group divides the partitions so that each is read by exactly one member, which makes the partition count the ceiling on parallelism: extra consumers sit idle. (Share groups, a separate group type that Kafka 4.2 made production-ready, let several consumers share a partition.) A member joining, leaving or failing to poll within max.poll.interval.ms triggers a rebalance. Adding partitions changes hash(key) % N, so keys move to new partitions and per-key order is not kept across the change.',
      'Committing the offset after processing gives at-least-once; committing before gives at-most-once. Kafka\'s exactly-once (since 0.11) combines an idempotent producer, where the broker drops resent batches by producer ID and sequence number, with transactions that write the output records and the consumer\'s offsets atomically, read by consumers set to read_committed.',
      'Exactly-once covers read-process-write inside Kafka. An HTTP call or a database write made by the processor can still happen twice; for an external store, keep the offset in the same store and transaction as the output, or make the write idempotent. Confluent\'s 2017 measurements on Kafka 0.11 put the transactional producer about 3% below an acks=all producer with one request in flight, and 20% below an acks=1 producer (1 KB messages, 100 ms transactions); Kafka Streams with a 100 ms commit interval lost 15–30% of its throughput.',
      'Event sourcing stores every change as an event and treats current state as derived: it can be rebuilt by replaying the log, queried as of any past time, and restored faster from a snapshot plus the later events. Replays must not repeat side effects, so calls to external systems go through a gateway that knows when it is replaying.',
      'Windows cut an unbounded stream into pieces: tumbling (no overlap), sliding (each event falls in size ÷ slide windows) and session (closed by a gap of inactivity). A watermark at t says no more events at or before t are expected; an operator\'s event time is the minimum over its inputs, so one idle partition stalls every window. Flink drops an event that arrives after the watermark has passed the end of its window (allowed lateness defaults to 0).'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Turning on exactly-once and assuming the whole pipeline is now exactly-once. The guarantee covers records and offsets inside Kafka; an email sent, a card charged or a row written to an outside database during processing can still be repeated after a crash.',
    say: 'I key the topic by account ID so each account\'s events stay ordered in one partition, with 48 partitions so that up to 48 consumers can share the work. Windows use event time with a watermark 30 s behind the newest event and an hour of allowed lateness, and the sink upserts by window and key so that a replay overwrites rather than double-counts.',
    refs: [
      ['kafka-intro', 'Order is per partition — “Kafka guarantees that any consumer of a given topic-partition will always read that partition’s events in exactly the same order as they were written.”'],
      ['kafka-consumer-javadoc', 'One consumer per partition — “This is achieved by balancing the partitions between all members in the consumer group so that each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in the group.”'],
      ['kafka-4-2-release', 'Share groups production-ready (Kafka 4.2, 2026) — “Kafka Queues (Share Groups) is now production-ready with new features like the RENEW acknowledgement type for extended processing times, adaptive batching for share coordinators, soft and strict enforcements of quantity of fetched records, and comprehensive lag metrics.”'],
      ['confluent-kafka-consumer', 'Extra consumers sit idle — “When consumer groups have too many consumers, extra consumers can sit idle, rebalances can happen more often and take longer, and the group coordinator can become overloaded.”'],
      ['kafka-operations', 'Adding partitions moves keys — “adding partitions doesn’t change the partitioning of existing data so this may disturb consumers if they rely on that partition.”'],
      ['kafka-design', 'Aborted records are only hidden — “Strictly speaking, the consumer doesn’t have to use read-committed isolation level, but if it does not, it will see records from aborted transactions and also open transactions which have not yet completed.”'],
      ['kafka-design', 'Offsets ride in the transaction — “we can write the offset to Kafka in the same transaction as the output topics receiving the processed data.”'],
      ['kafka-streams-upgrade-41', 'The EOS v2 setting — “This can be configured by setting "processing.guarantee" to "exactly_once_v2" for application versions 3.0+, or setting it to "exactly_once_beta" for versions between 2.6 and 2.8.”'],
      ['kafka-streams-upgrade-41', 'Kafka Streams 4.0 — “In this release, eos-v1 (Exactly Once Semantics version 1) is no longer supported.”'],
      ['confluent-exactly-once', 'Producer overhead (Narkhede and Wang, 2017) — “For 1 KB messages and transactions lasting 100 ms, the producer throughput declines only by 3%, compared to the throughput of a producer configured for at least once, in-order delivery (acks=all, max.in.flight.requests.per.connection=1), and by 20% compared to the throughput of a producer configured for most once delivery with no ordering guarantees (acks=1, max.in.flight.requests.per.connection=5), which is the current default.”'],
      ['confluent-exactly-once', 'Streams overhead (Narkhede and Wang, 2017) — “With a short commit interval of 100 ms—that is required to keep end-to-end latency low—we see a throughput degradation of 15% to 30% depending on the message size, which is a 1 KB message size for the former and 100 bytes for the latter.”'],
      ['confluent-exactly-once', 'Where exactly-once stops (Narkhede and Wang, 2017) — “if the event streaming app written in Streams makes an RPC call to update some remote stores, or if it uses a customized client to directly read or write to a Kafka topic, the resulting side effects would not be guaranteed exactly once.”'],
      ['fowler-event-sourcing', 'Replays and external systems — “if these events cause update messages to be sent to external systems, then things will go wrong because those external systems don\'t know the difference between real processing and replays.”'],
      ['flink-time-concepts', 'Event time is the slowest input — “Such an operator’s current event time is the minimum of its input streams’ event times.”'],
      ['flink-watermarks', 'Idle partitions — “the watermark will be held back, because it is computed as the minimum over all the different parallel watermarks.”'],
      ['flink-windows', 'Late means past the window end — “By default, late elements are dropped when the watermark is past the end of the window.”'],
      ['flink-windows', 'Late data is dropped by default — “By default, the allowed lateness is set to 0. That is, elements that arrive behind the watermark will be dropped.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['What limits how many consumers in one consumer group can do useful work on a Kafka topic?', 'The partition count. Each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in the group, so with 12 partitions a 13th consumer sits idle. To go further you add partitions, which remaps keys to partitions for new data.'],
      ['What exactly does Kafka\'s exactly-once cover?', 'A read-process-write cycle within Kafka: the idempotent producer removes duplicates caused by retries, and a transaction commits the output records and the consumer\'s offsets together, visible to read_committed consumers. Side effects outside Kafka, such as RPCs or database writes, are not covered.'],
      ['What is a watermark, and why can one idle partition stall a job?', 'A watermark at time t declares that no more events with timestamps at or before t are expected, so windows ending before t can fire. An operator takes the minimum watermark of its inputs, so an input that stops sending holds everything back unless it is marked idle.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A topic has 12 partitions and takes in 30,000 messages/s. Each consumer handles 2,000 messages/s, and lag is growing with 12 consumers in a consumer group (not a share group). The team scales the group to 24 consumers. What happens to the lag?', ['It keeps growing: 12 consumers sit idle and capacity stays 24,000/s', 'It drains at 18,000/s, because the group\'s capacity doubles to 48,000/s', 'It drains, because Kafka splits each partition between two consumers now', 'It drains, but each message is now processed by two consumers in the group'], 'Each partition goes to exactly one consumer in a group, so only 12 of the 24 get work: 12 × 2,000 = 24,000/s against 30,000/s arriving, and lag still grows by 6,000/s. The doubling sum forgets that cap. More capacity needs more partitions (at least 30,000 ÷ 2,000 = 15, more for headroom) or faster consumers.', ['kafka-consumer-javadoc', 'confluent-kafka-consumer']],
      ['A Kafka Streams app runs with processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2. For each payment it calls an external fraud API over HTTP, then writes a result to an output topic. An instance crashes mid-transaction and another takes over. What can happen?', ['No duplicates for read_committed readers, but some payments hit the fraud API twice', 'No duplicates anywhere, because one transaction covers both the topic and the API', 'Duplicate results in the topic, visible to read_committed consumers downstream', 'Some payments skipped, because the aborted run had committed their offsets'], 'The aborted transaction\'s output is never shown to read_committed consumers and its offsets roll back, so the new instance reprocesses those payments and read_committed consumers see one result each (the aborted records stay in the log, hidden from them). The HTTP calls already happened and are made again: Confluent states that RPCs to remote stores are outside the guarantee. Make the external call idempotent, for example keyed by payment ID.', ['confluent-exactly-once', 'kafka-design']],
      ['A Flink job reads a 16-partition topic and computes one-minute event-time windows. After a producer change, partition 7 stops receiving events while the other 15 stay busy. Soon no window fires for any key. Why?', ['The watermark is a minimum over partitions, so idle partition 7 holds it back', 'Partition 7\'s consumer crashed, and a rebalance loop now stops the other consumers', 'Allowed lateness is 0, so events from the other 15 partitions are dropped as late', 'Event-time windows fire on the processing clock, which stops when one input idles'], 'Watermarks are generated per partition and merged by taking the minimum, so a partition with no events never advances and holds the job\'s event time back. Marking idle inputs (withIdleness) lets the busy partitions move the watermark on. The late-data option has it backwards: with a stalled watermark nothing is late; everything is waiting.', ['flink-watermarks', 'flink-time-concepts']],
      ['Hourly revenue windows run on event time with a watermark 5 minutes behind the newest event. About 1% of sales come from shop terminals that upload up to 2 hours late, and finance needs them counted. With Flink\'s defaults, what happens to those sales, and what fixes it?', ['Dropped; 2 hours of allowed lateness counts them, with 2 hours more window state', 'Counted; Flink\'s default allowed lateness is one window length, here an hour', 'Counted; the watermark waits until the terminals have uploaded their sales', 'Dropped; a 2-hour watermark delay counts them and keeps hourly results just as timely'], 'Allowed lateness defaults to 0, so a sale that arrives after the watermark has passed its window\'s end is dropped. Allowed lateness keeps each window\'s state until the watermark passes the window end plus the lateness, and fires the window again when a straggler arrives, so finance gets an on-time result and a corrected one. A 2-hour watermark delay also counts them, but makes every hourly result 2 hours late.', ['flink-windows']],
      ['An event-sourced order service fixes a bug in a projection and rebuilds its read model by replaying three years of events. The handler for OrderShipped also sends the customer an email. What happens, and what is the usual remedy?', ['Customers are emailed again; route it through a gateway that is off during replay', 'Nothing, as replays skip handlers; take a snapshot first so the rebuild runs faster', 'Events apply out of order; replay one partition at a time to keep the sequence', 'Old events are refused as already acknowledged; reset the consumer offsets first'], 'Replaying runs the same handlers, and the email system cannot tell a replay from real processing. Fowler\'s answer is to wrap external systems in a gateway that can be switched off, or made replay-aware, during rebuilds. A snapshot only shortens the replay; it does not stop handlers running. A replay applies events in their stored sequence, which is why the rebuilt state matches. Stored events are not refused for having been processed before: rebuilding means reading them again from the start, and that is what makes the email handler run again.', ['fowler-event-sourcing']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how a log-based stream processor such as Kafka with Flink scales, what delivery guarantees it gives, and how it handles time.', ['Partitions: per-key order, one consumer per partition, partition count caps parallelism', 'Offsets and retention allow replay; commit order decides at-least-once or at-most-once', 'Exactly-once = idempotent producer + transactions, and it stops at external side effects', 'Event sourcing: state rebuilt from events, with side effects guarded during replay', 'Event time, windows, watermarks and allowed lateness']],
    duck: ['If Kafka keeps the messages after I read them, how does it know what I have already processed?', 'Why can\'t the system just wait until all the late events have arrived?'],
    lab: { type: 'seq', parts: [
      { type: 'estimate', brief: 'Size a stream job.', fixed: [
        { q: 'Ingress is 45,000 messages/s and one consumer handles 3,000 messages/s. You want room for traffic to double. How many partitions?', a: 30, unit: 'partitions', tol: 0, how: '45,000 × 2 ÷ 3,000 = 30. Partitions cap the consumers in a group, and adding them later remaps keys, so size for growth up front.' },
        { q: '50,000 events/s feed 10-minute windows that slide every minute. How many window updates per second?', a: 500000, unit: 'updates/s', tol: 0.05, how: 'Each event lands in 10 ÷ 1 = 10 windows: 50,000 × 10 = 500,000.' },
        { q: '20,000 messages/s of 1 KB each, kept for 7 days with replication factor 3. How much disk, in TB?', a: 36.3, unit: 'TB', tol: 0.1, how: '20,000 × 1 KB = 20 MB/s × 604,800 s ≈ 12.1 TB, × 3 replicas ≈ 36.3 TB.' },
        { q: 'The watermark trails the newest event time by 5 s. A window covers 12:00:00–12:01:00. What newest event time first lets it fire, in seconds after 12:00:00?', a: 65, unit: 's', tol: 0.02, how: 'It fires when the watermark reaches 12:01:00, which needs an event stamped 12:01:05: 65 s after 12:00:00.' }
      ] },
      { type: 'sort', brief: 'Which part of the stream design deals with this?', buckets: ['Partitions and consumer groups', 'Delivery guarantees', 'Event time and windows'], items: [
        ['Events for one customer go to one partition by key', 0], ['Consumers beyond the partition count sit idle', 0], ['Adding partitions remaps hash(key) % N', 0],
        ['Producer ID and sequence numbers drop resent batches', 1], ['Offsets committed in the same transaction as the output', 1], ['Committing offsets before processing risks losing messages', 1],
        ['A watermark says no more events at or before time t', 2], ['Session windows close after a gap of inactivity', 2], ['An idle partition holds back event time', 2], ['Allowed lateness keeps window state open for stragglers', 2]
      ] }
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '3.3', mod: '3', title: 'Pub/sub', tag: 'Every subscription gets its own copy',
    analogy: 'A newspaper with postal subscribers. Each household gets its own copy in its own letterbox, so one family leaving theirs unread for a week changes nothing for the neighbours. Inside a household the family shares one copy, and whoever picks it up reads it.',
    points: [
      'A topic delivers every message to every subscription, and each subscription is its own queue with its own backlog and acknowledgements. Subscribers attached to one subscription share its messages; separate subscriptions each get everything published after they were created (and earlier messages only if the topic retains them and the subscription seeks back).',
      'Give every consumer its own buffer. With SNS to SQS, each subscribed queue keeps the messages until that consumer processes them, so a slow or offline consumer falls behind alone. SNS pushing straight to an HTTP/S endpoint retries for at most an hour, then discards the message unless a dead-letter queue is attached.',
      'Delivery is at least once by default: Pub/Sub can redeliver a message even after the ack succeeded, so handlers must be idempotent. Redis Pub/Sub is at most once: a subscriber that errors or disconnects loses the message for good, which suits only signals you can afford to miss.',
      'Global order means one sequence and no parallelism, so systems order per key. Pub/Sub ordering keys keep order within a key and none across keys; each key is limited to 1 MBps of publishing, and ordered delivery lowers publish availability and raises latency. Redelivering one message for a key redelivers every later message for that key, even acknowledged ones.',
      'Filter at the broker rather than in every subscriber. A Pub/Sub subscription filter matches attributes only, not the message body; messages that do not match are acknowledged by Pub/Sub itself, and the subscriber pays no outbound message fee for them (delivery fees still apply).',
      'Push or pull. A push subscription POSTs each message to an HTTPS endpoint and adjusts its rate to the success responses, which suits serverless handlers; a pull subscriber asks for messages at its own pace. With ordering on, push allows only one outstanding message per key, so a busy key is limited to one request round trip at a time.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Broadcasting without a buffer per subscriber. On Redis Pub/Sub, or with SNS pushing straight to an HTTP endpoint that has no dead-letter queue, a subscriber that is down for longer than the retries never receives those messages.',
    say: 'Order events go to one topic with a subscription each for billing, search indexing and email, so the indexer can fall an hour behind without touching billing. I need order only within an order, so the order ID is the ordering key, and handlers dedupe on event ID because delivery is at least once.',
    refs: [
      ['gcp-pubsub-overview', 'Each subscription gets a copy — “Each subscription receives a copy of A and B messages from the topic.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-overview', 'Subscribers on one subscription share — “Each of the subscriber applications gets a subset of the published messages, and no two subscriber applications get the same subset of messages.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-subscriptions', 'Replaying older messages — “The topic retention feature lets a subscription attached to a topic seek back in time and replay previously published messages.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-subscriptions', 'Redelivery after a successful ack — “Pub/Sub might redeliver a message even after an acknowledgment request for the message returns successfully.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-ordering', 'Per-key throughput — “The publishing throughput on each ordering key is limited to 1 MBps.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-ordering', 'Redelivery under ordering — “Redeliveries of a message trigger redelivery of all subsequent messages for that key, even acknowledged ones.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-ordering', 'Dead-letter topics and order — “Be aware that order might not be preserved when messages are written to a dead-letter topic.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-push', 'Push adapts to the subscriber — “Pub/Sub dynamically adjusts the rate of push requests based on the rate at which it receives success responses.”'],
      ['gcp-pubsub-filtering', 'Filters see attributes only — “You can filter messages by their attributes, but not by the data in the message.”'],
      ['aws-sns-sqs-fanout', 'A queue per consumer — “The messages also persist in an Amazon SQS queue for other applications to process later.”'],
      ['aws-sns-retries', 'When retries run out — “When the delivery policy is exhausted, Amazon SNS stops retrying the delivery and discards the message—unless a dead-letter queue is attached to the subscription.”'],
      ['redis-pubsub', 'At most once — “If the subscriber is unable to handle the message (for example, due to an error or a network disconnect) the message is forever lost.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['What is the difference between adding a subscriber and adding a subscription?', 'A new subscriber on an existing subscription shares that subscription\'s messages with the others (load balancing). A new subscription gets its own copy of every message published after it was created (fan-out); it sees earlier messages only by seeking back on a topic with retention.'],
      ['What does a Pub/Sub ordering key buy, and what does it cost?', 'Messages with the same key arrive in publish order; different keys have no relative order. The cost: 1 MBps of publishing per key, one outstanding batch (pull) or message (push) per key, lower publish availability, higher latency, and redelivery of every later message for a key when one is redelivered.'],
      ['When is Redis Pub/Sub the wrong tool?', 'When a missed message matters. It is at-most-once: if a subscriber is disconnected or fails, the message is lost for good. Use a durable log or queue (Redis Streams, Kafka, SQS) for anything that must arrive.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['Billing, search indexing and email each need every OrderPlaced event. To keep things simple, the workers of all three services are attached to one Pub/Sub subscription on the order topic. What happens?', ['Each event reaches just one of the three services', 'Each service sees each event, since the topic fans out to subscribers', 'Each service receives each event three times, once per service', 'Pub/Sub rejects the second service that tries to attach to it'], 'Subscribers on one subscription split its messages between them, so billing, indexing and email each see a fraction of the events. Fan-out comes from subscriptions: give each service its own and each gets a copy of every message. Topics do fan out, but per subscription, not per subscriber.', ['gcp-pubsub-overview']],
      ['To get a single global order, a team publishes every event to Pub/Sub with the same ordering key, "all". Events average 2 KB and the target is 2,000 events/s. At most how many events/s can they publish?', ['About 500 events/s, a quarter of the target', 'About 1,000 events/s, half of the target', 'About 2,000 events/s, set by the topic quota', 'About 4,000 events/s, twice the target rate'], '1 MBps per ordering key ÷ 2 KB per event ≈ 500 events/s. 1,000 assumes 1 KB events; 2,000 applies the topic-wide quota, which helps only when traffic is spread over many keys; 4,000 is the KB/s the target needs, read as events. Per-entity keys, such as the account ID, keep the order that matters and spread the load.', ['gcp-pubsub-ordering']],
      ['A Pub/Sub subscription has message ordering on. The subscriber receives messages 1, 2 and 3 for key K and acknowledges all three, but the ack for message 2 does not persist and it is redelivered. What does the subscriber receive?', ['Message 2, then message 3 again, though 3 was acknowledged', 'Message 2 alone, because message 3 was already acknowledged', 'Nothing more, because ordered subscriptions are exactly-once', 'Message 2 alone, delivered to the dead-letter topic instead'], 'To keep the key in order, Pub/Sub redelivers every later message for that key along with the redelivered one, even messages already acknowledged. Ordered handlers must therefore be idempotent too: processing 3 a second time has to be harmless.', ['gcp-pubsub-ordering']],
      ['Thirty cache nodes subscribe to a Redis Pub/Sub channel that announces which product keys to invalidate. Cache entries have a 10-minute TTL. One node loses its connection to Redis for 20 s during a burst of price changes, and keeps its cached entries. What is the result?', ['It misses those messages and serves stale prices until the entries expire', 'Redis buffers the node\'s messages and delivers them when it reconnects', 'Redis makes the publisher wait until the dropped node has resubscribed', 'The node fetches the messages it missed from another subscriber on rejoining'], 'Redis Pub/Sub is at-most-once: a message is sent once, and a subscriber that is disconnected never gets it. For invalidations that must arrive, use a durable stream that the node can resume from (Redis Streams or Kafka), and keep a TTL on cache entries as a backstop.', ['redis-pubsub']],
      ['An SNS topic pushes straight to a partner\'s HTTPS endpoint, and the subscription has no dead-letter queue. The partner\'s service is down for 10 hours. What happens to the messages published while it is down?', ['They are discarded once retries run out, within an hour', 'They wait in the topic for up to 14 days, as SQS would', 'They are retried for 23 days, so they arrive later', 'They block publishing to the topic until it recovers'], 'An HTTP/S delivery policy can retry for at most 3,600 seconds, and when it is exhausted SNS discards the message unless a DLQ is attached. The 23-day schedule belongs to AWS-managed endpoints such as SQS, which is why subscribing an SQS queue, and having the partner consume from it, gives a buffer that outlasts the outage.', ['aws-sns-retries', 'aws-sns-sqs-fanout']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how you would use pub/sub to send one stream of events to several independent consumers, and what ordering and delivery guarantees they get.', ['A topic fans out to subscriptions; subscribers within one subscription share the load', 'A buffer per consumer (subscription or queue) isolates slow or offline consumers', 'At-least-once by default, so idempotent handlers; Redis Pub/Sub is at-most-once', 'Order per key, not global, and what ordering costs', 'Broker-side filters, and push versus pull delivery']],
    duck: ['If everyone subscribes to the same topic, why doesn\'t a slow subscriber hold up the fast ones?', 'Why not put everything under one ordering key so that it all stays in order?'],
    lab: { type: 'seq', parts: [
      { type: 'sort', brief: 'Which setup gives this?', buckets: ['Separate subscription (fan-out)', 'Shared subscription (load balancing)', 'Subscription filter'], items: [
        ['Billing and email both need every order event', 0], ['Add an analytics consumer without touching the others', 0], ['A slow search indexer must not delay billing', 0],
        ['Ten replicas of the email service share the work', 1], ['Scale the indexer from 4 to 12 workers for a backfill', 1], ['Each order email is handled by just one of the workers', 1],
        ['The EU warehouse wants only events whose region attribute is EU', 2], ['The SMS sender only wants events with priority=high', 2], ['Skip outbound message fees for events a consumer would discard', 2]
      ] },
      { type: 'pick', brief: 'Order events feed billing, search indexing and email. Pick the four choices that fit.', want: 4, options: [
        ['A separate subscription for each of the three services', true, 'Each service needs every event, and each gets its own backlog.'],
        ['The order ID as the ordering key, rather than one key', true, 'Order matters within an order, and many keys keep throughput.'],
        ['A dead-letter topic on each subscription, with an alarm', true, 'A message that always fails is set aside, not retried for ever; with ordering keys this breaks that key\'s order, so handlers must tolerate the gap.'],
        ['Handlers that dedupe on event ID, as delivery is at least once', true, 'Pub/Sub can redeliver a message even after a successful ack.'],
        ['One shared subscription, so each event is handled only once', false, 'Each event would reach only one of the three services.'],
        ['Redis Pub/Sub for billing, since it has the lowest latency', false, 'It is at-most-once: a disconnected subscriber loses messages.'],
        ['One ordering key for every event, so that totals stay consistent', false, 'One key caps publishing at 1 MBps and removes all parallelism.'],
        ['Add the email subscription next month; it will pick up earlier events', false, 'A new subscription starts empty; it gets earlier messages only if topic retention is on and it seeks back.']
      ] }
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '3.4', mod: '3', title: 'Push vs pull dataflow', tag: 'Do the work when posting, or when reading',
    analogy: 'A club newsletter. You can post a copy to every member the day it is written, which is slow for a big club but lets each member just open the letterbox; or pin one copy on the club\'s noticeboard and let each member visit every board they care about, which is cheap to publish and slow to read.',
    points: [
      'Fan-out on write (push) copies each new post into every follower\'s precomputed timeline, so a read is one lookup and a post costs one write per follower. At Twitter in 2012–13, home timelines were lists of tweet entries (tweet ID, author ID and flag bits) in Redis, capped at 800 entries and kept on 3 machines, and a tweet to 20K followers meant 20K inserts.',
      'Fan-out on read (pull) stores each post once, under its author, and builds the feed at request time by fetching and merging the recent posts of everyone the reader follows. A post is one write; a read costs one fetch per followee. Facebook\'s Multifeed (described in 2015) works this way: when a user opens the feed it looks up their friends, gathers their recent actions and ranks them.',
      'Compare total work: push costs posts/s × followers per post, pull costs reads/s × followees per read. If posting and reading were spread evenly over users, both averages would be edges ÷ users and the ratio would be the read:write ratio; real activity is skewed (big accounts post to huge audiences), so that is only a first guess. Twitter served about 300K timeline queries/s (home and search) against 6K writes/s, and pushed home timelines.',
      'Skew breaks pure push. With Lady Gaga\'s 31 million followers a tweet could take up to 5 minutes to reach everyone, and replies reached people before the original. Twitter was moving to a hybrid that stops fanning out the largest accounts and merges their recent tweets in at read time, which the talk (as summarised by High Scalability) said saves tens of percent of compute.',
      'Precomputed feeds cost more than write volume. LinkedIn (2016) chose fan-out on read for FollowFeed: the pushed copies made the data 62 times larger, and a feed stored in ranked order has to be rewritten whenever the ranking model changes, which slows A/B tests of new models.',
      'The same choice recurs. Twitter\'s search is the pull extreme: a tweet is written to one index shard, and every query asks all shards and merges, about 100 ms a read. Push cost is per author and pull cost is per reader: a celebrity makes posting expensive, and a reader who follows thousands makes reading expensive.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Fanning out on write for every account because reads dominate on average. The few accounts with millions of followers can produce most of the timeline writes, take minutes to deliver, and make followers see a conversation out of order; the averages hide them.',
    say: 'Reads outnumber posts about 50 to 1, so I push: 1,000 posts/s × 200 followers is 200K timeline inserts/s. Accounts above a threshold, say a million followers, are not fanned out; their recent posts are fetched and merged at read time, which adds a handful of lookups to each read.',
    refs: [
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'Twitter 2012–13 (High Scalability summary): one insert per follower — “So for every write of a tweet as many as 20K inserts are occurring across the Redis cluster.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'Reads and writes — “At 150 million users with 300K QPS for timelines (home and search) naive materialization can be slow.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'What an entry holds — “What is being stored is the tweet ID of the generated tweet, the user ID of the originator of the tweet, and 4 bytes of bits used to mark if it’s a retweet or a reply or something else.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'Timelines are capped — “Your home timeline sits in a Redis cluster and has a maximum of 800 entries.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'Three copies of each timeline — “Effectively running 3 different hash rings because your timeline is in 3 different places.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'Slow fan-out reorders conversations — “Replies are being seen all the time before the original tweets for celebrities.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'A change in progress — “Twitter is changing from doing all the work on writes to doing more work on reads for high value users.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'What the hybrid saved — “Balances read and write paths. Saves 10s of percents of computational resources.”'],
      ['highscalability-twitter-timelines', 'Search is the inverse — “In fanout a tweet may be stored in N home timelines of how many people are following you, in Early Bird a tweet is only stored in one Early Bird machine (except for replication).”'],
      ['linkedin-followfeed', 'LinkedIn, 2016: storage — “In our experiments, we found out that data size with this model was 62 times greater than pull based architecture.”'],
      ['linkedin-followfeed', 'LinkedIn, 2016: ranking changes — “This grandfathering step can be IO intensive, thus it can be slow and reduce the velocity of overall iteration and A/B testing.”'],
      ['fb-multifeed', 'Facebook, 2015: built at read time — “When a person goes to his or her Facebook feed, Multifeed looks up the user’s friends, finds all their recent actions, and decides what should be rendered based on a certain relevance and ranking algorithm.”'],
      ['fb-multifeed', 'Facebook, 2015: the expensive reader — “It was not uncommon for an aggregator to get a heavy request from a user with a lot of friends, leading to a sudden spike in CPU usage.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['How does the cost of fan-out on write and fan-out on read scale?', 'On write: each post costs one insert per follower of its author, and a read is one lookup. On read: each post is one write, and each read costs one fetch per account the reader follows, plus a merge.'],
      ['How does the hybrid read a home timeline?', 'It reads the reader\'s precomputed timeline, which holds posts from ordinary accounts, fetches the recent posts of the few high-follower accounts the reader follows, and merges the two by time (or by score).'],
      ['Why did LinkedIn choose fan-out on read for its feed in 2016?', 'Materialising each viewer\'s feed made the data 62 times larger, and because feeds are ranked, every new ranking model would mean rewriting every stored feed, which slowed A/B testing. Building the feed at read time, with ranking next to the data on index nodes, avoided both.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A network handles 2,000 posts/s, and an author has 300 followers on average. It serves 50,000 home-timeline reads/s, and a reader follows 300 accounts on average. How does the number of timeline operations per second under pure fan-out on read (fetches) compare with pure fan-out on write (inserts)?', ['About 25 times more', 'About 300 times more', 'About 7,500 times more', 'About 25 times less'], 'Push: 2,000 × 300 = 600,000 timeline writes/s. Pull: 50,000 × 300 = 15,000,000 timeline fetches/s, 25 times as many, which is just the read:write ratio because the stem gives equal averages. 300 divides pull work by the read rate instead of by the push work; 7,500 forgets to multiply posts by followers; "25 times less" inverts the ratio.', 'calc'],
      ['Under pure fan-out on write, an account with 30 million followers tweets and followers start replying within seconds. Twitter described a user-visible problem this caused. Which?', ['Some followers see replies before the tweet they reply to', 'Followers see the tweet twice, once per fan-out attempt', 'Its fan-out stops at 800 timelines, the timeline length cap', 'Replies are held back until the original is delivered'], 'Fanning one tweet out to tens of millions of timelines could take minutes, while a reply from an early recipient fanned out to its own smaller audience quickly, so some people saw the reply first. The 800-entry cap limits how many tweets each timeline keeps, not how many timelines a tweet reaches. Problems like this are why Twitter moved to stop fanning out its largest accounts.', ['highscalability-twitter-timelines']],
      ['LinkedIn ranks every member\'s feed with an ML model. Its 2016 FollowFeed write-up explains why it chose fan-out on read over pre-materialised feeds. Which reasons did it give?', ['Copies multiplied the stored data, and model changes meant re-ranking stored feeds', 'Pull reads beat a precomputed-feed lookup, so latency decided the design', 'Members posted more often than they read, so the write path was the bottleneck', 'Kafka could not carry the event volume, and fan-out jobs overran their window'], 'LinkedIn measured 62 times the data with fan-out on write, and a feed stored in ranked order must be re-ranked and rewritten for every new model, which is IO-heavy and slows A/B testing. Its low latency came from putting the ranking computation next to the data on index nodes, not from pull being faster than a lookup.', ['linkedin-followfeed']],
      ['Home timelines hold up to 800 tweet IDs of 8 bytes each, and every timeline is kept on 3 machines. Roughly how much RAM do timelines for 150 million active users need, before overhead?', ['About 2.9 TB', 'About 0.96 TB', 'About 23 TB', 'About 0.36 TB'], '150,000,000 × 800 × 8 bytes = 0.96 TB, × 3 copies ≈ 2.9 TB. Twitter\'s real entries also held the author\'s user ID and 4 bytes of flags, so a full timeline took more than this. 0.96 TB forgets the copies; 0.36 TB counts entries rather than bytes; 23 TB multiplies by 8 a second time, as if converting from bits.', 'calc'],
      ['A feed is built on read: when a user opens it, an aggregator gathers recent activity from the user\'s network and ranks it. Which users make the most expensive feed requests?', ['Users who follow a great many accounts', 'Users whose accounts have the most followers', 'Users who post many times over each day', 'Users who signed up for the service today'], 'On read, a request costs one fetch per account the reader follows; an author\'s follower count costs nothing until someone reads. Facebook reported aggregator CPU spikes from requests by users with many friends. Follower count is the expensive dimension for fan-out on write, the opposite model.', ['fb-multifeed']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain fan-out on write versus fan-out on read for a social feed, how you would choose between them, and why large systems mix them.', ['Push: copy each post into followers\' timelines; cheap reads, cost per follower', 'Pull: store once and merge followees at read time; cheap writes, cost per followee', 'Choose by the read:write ratio and the follower distribution, with numbers', 'Hybrid: skip fan-out for high-follower accounts and merge them at read time', 'Other costs: storage for copies, rewriting on ranking changes; the same choice in search']],
    duck: ['Why not just look up everyone I follow when I open the app?', 'If copying every post to every follower is so expensive, why does anyone do it?'],
    lab: { type: 'seq', parts: [
      { type: 'estimate', brief: 'Size the fan-out.', fixed: [
        { q: '300 posts/s, and an author has 400 followers on average. Timeline inserts per second under pure fan-out on write?', a: 120000, unit: 'inserts/s', tol: 0.05, how: '300 × 400 = 120,000.' },
        { q: 'Of 300 posts/s, 0.03 posts/s come from accounts averaging 20 million followers; the rest average 400. What share of all inserts comes from those big accounts?', a: 83, unit: '%', tol: 0.05, how: 'Big: 0.03 × 20,000,000 = 600,000/s. Rest: 299.97 × 400 ≈ 120,000/s. 600,000 ÷ 720,000 ≈ 83%. One post in ten thousand makes most of the write load.' },
        { q: 'Under fan-out on read, 40,000 timeline reads/s each fetch the recent posts of 250 followed accounts. Fetches per second?', a: 10000000, unit: 'fetches/s', tol: 0.05, how: '40,000 × 250 = 10 million, against one lookup per read when timelines are precomputed.' },
        { q: 'Under the hybrid, a user follows 500 accounts, 4 of them above the fan-out threshold. How many timelines does one read of their home timeline touch?', a: 5, unit: 'timelines', tol: 0, how: 'Their own precomputed timeline plus the 4 big accounts\' timelines, merged: 1 + 4 = 5, instead of 500 under pure fan-out on read.' }
      ] },
      { type: 'sort', brief: 'Which model is this?', buckets: ['Fan-out on write (push)', 'Fan-out on read (pull)'], items: [
        ['A post costs one write per follower', 0], ['Reads are a single lookup of a precomputed list', 0], ['A new ranking model means rewriting stored feeds', 0], ['A celebrity post can take minutes to reach every follower', 0], ['Storage grows with posts × followers', 0],
        ['A read costs one fetch per account followed', 1], ['A post is stored once, under its author', 1], ['Twitter search: one shard written, every shard queried', 1], ['A reader who follows thousands makes reads expensive', 1], ['Facebook\'s Multifeed builds the feed when the user opens it', 1]
      ] }
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '3.5', mod: '3', title: 'CQRS', tag: 'One model to change data, others to read',
    analogy: 'A library\'s accessions desk and its catalogues. Every new book is checked and registered once at the desk; the author, title and subject catalogues are separate indexes built from those registrations, each arranged for one way of looking things up. A book registered this morning may not reach the subject catalogue until the afternoon.',
    points: [
      'CQRS splits the model that changes data from the models that answer queries. Commands ("book room 12") run through validation and business rules on the write model; queries read DTOs from read models that hold no domain logic.',
      'The split can stop at the code, over one shared database, or go as far as separate stores: a normalised relational write store and read stores of any shape (a document per customer, a search index, a key-value table), each scaled for its own load.',
      'Read stores are kept up to date by projections fed from events the write side publishes or from change data capture on its log. Publishing as a second step after the commit is a dual write; use an outbox row in the same transaction, and make each projection skip events it has already applied, by tracking the last sequence number it processed.',
      'Read models lag, so a user can save and then not see the change. Answer the item a user has just changed from the write side, or have the command return the event\'s sequence number and read only once the projection has passed it, as Berkeley DB does with commit tokens for replicas (topic 2.4). A search-index projector should record its position only once the write is searchable (Elasticsearch: refresh=wait_for).',
      'A plain read replica (topic 2.4) is the simplest separate read store, and Azure allows it, but it keeps the same schema and serves the same queries; a read model takes its own shape and technology, and can be thrown away and rebuilt from the source. Not the same as event sourcing either, which is one way to store the write side; event stores are generally read as one stream per entity, so they need projections for everything else.',
      'Most systems should not use it. For a simple domain with CRUD screens it adds a synchronisation pipeline and visible lag for nothing; a reporting database for the few heavy queries is the lighter tool.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Committing the change and then publishing the event as a separate step. A crash between the two leaves a read model that never hears about the change, and nothing reports an error; publishing first instead announces changes that may roll back.',
    say: 'Orders are written to a normalised Postgres write model; an outbox table read by change data capture feeds two projections, a search index and a per-customer order history, and we alert if either falls more than a few seconds behind. A user\'s own just-placed order is read from the write side, so they never see the lag.',
    refs: [
      ['fowler-cqrs', 'Different models — “At its heart is the notion that you can use a different model to update information than the model you use to read information.”'],
      ['fowler-cqrs', 'Usually not worth it — “For some situations, this separation can be valuable, but beware that for most systems CQRS adds risky complexity.”'],
      ['azure-cqrs', 'Separate stores — “Separation of the read and write data stores allows you to scale each model to match the load. It also enables you to use a different storage technology for each data store.”'],
      ['azure-cqrs', 'Replica or new shape — “The read data store can be a read-only replica of the write store or have a different structure.”'],
      ['azure-cqrs', 'The dual-write problem — “Because you usually can\'t enlist message brokers and databases into a single distributed transaction, consistency problems can occur when you update the database and publish events.”'],
      ['azure-cqrs', 'Outbox and idempotent consumers — “Use the Transactional Outbox pattern to persist the state change and event atomically, and make the read-model consumer idempotent to tolerate duplicate delivery.”'],
      ['azure-cqrs', 'Lag — “When the read databases and write databases are separated, the read data might not show the most recent changes immediately.”'],
      ['azure-materialized-view', 'Read models are disposable — “A key point is that a materialized view and the data it contains are completely disposable because they can be entirely rebuilt from the source data stores.”'],
      ['azure-event-sourcing', 'Idempotent projections — “Track the last processed event sequence number for each consumer and skip duplicates, or design state mutations that are inherently safe to repeat.”'],
      ['azure-event-sourcing', 'Event stores are read by ID — “The only data that you can extract is a stream of events by using an event identifier as the criteria.”'],
      ['bdb-read-your-writes', 'Read-your-writes by token (Berkeley DB) — “Later, the application running on a client site can use a copy of the token to determine whether the transaction has been applied at that site.”'],
      ['es-refresh-parameter', 'Waiting until searchable — “Wait for the changes made by the request to be made visible by a refresh before replying.”'],
      ['debezium-outbox-blog', 'Outbox plus CDC — “Log-based Change Data Capture (CDC) is a great fit for capturing new entries in the outbox table and stream them to Apache Kafka.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['How does a CQRS read model differ from a read replica?', 'A replica copies the same schema and serves the same queries, so it scales reads of one shape; Azure lists it as one option for the read store. A read model is built for particular queries and can use a different technology (a search index, a document store).'],
      ['Does CQRS require event sourcing?', 'No. CQRS separates the models for writing and reading; event sourcing is one way of storing the write side, as an append-only log of events. They pair well because an event store is generally read as one stream per entity, so every other query needs a projection.'],
      ['How do you give a user read-your-writes when the read model lags?', 'Serve the item they just changed from the write side, or return the write\'s sequence number with the command\'s response and have the read wait until the projection has processed that number (for a search index, until the write is also searchable).']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A projector applies 2,500 events/s to a read model. Events arrive at 2,000/s at peak. The projector is down for 10 minutes at peak and peak continues. How long after restart until the read model has caught up?', ['40 minutes', '8 minutes', '10 minutes', '50 minutes'], 'Backlog: 2,000 × 600 s = 1.2M events. New events keep arriving, so the backlog drains at only 2,500 − 2,000 = 500/s: 1.2M ÷ 500 = 2,400 s = 40 min. Eight minutes divides by the full 2,500/s and forgets the arrivals; 50 minutes builds the backlog at the projector\'s rate instead of the arrival rate.', 'calc'],
      ['The order service commits the order row, then publishes OrderPlaced to the broker in a second call. A pod is killed between the two. Read models are built from the broker\'s events. What follows, and what is the fix?', ['The order exists but no read model shows it; add an outbox row in the same transaction', 'The broker redelivers it after the restart; make the read-model consumers idempotent', 'The database rolls the order back, as no event went out; retry the whole request', 'The pod resends it when it restarts; give the producer a longer retry timeout'], 'The commit succeeded and the event was never sent, so nothing will ever deliver it: the broker never had it to redeliver, and the unsent event lived only in the killed pod\'s memory. A database and a broker cannot share one transaction, so the event is written to an outbox table inside the order\'s transaction and relayed afterwards, by polling or by change data capture. Idempotent consumers are still needed for the relay\'s repeats, but they cannot recover an event that was never sent.', ['azure-cqrs', 'debezium-outbox-blog']],
      ['A user edits a listing title and is redirected to "My listings", which reads a search-index projection that lags 1–3 s. They see the old title. Which fix is precise without sending every read to the write store?', ['Return the event\'s sequence number; read once the projection has passed it', 'Add more replicas to the search cluster so that it answers the query faster', 'Pause 3 seconds after each write before redirecting the user to the list', 'Update the search index inside the same database transaction as the edit'], 'The projection records the last sequence number it applied, so the read can check whether the user\'s write is in yet, as Berkeley DB clients do with commit tokens. A search projector must record that number only once the write is searchable (refresh=wait_for), or the check passes while search still shows the old title. A fixed pause guesses at the lag and fails when it grows; more search replicas speed up queries, not the projection; and a search index cannot be enlisted in the database\'s transaction.', ['azure-event-sourcing', 'bdb-read-your-writes', 'es-refresh-parameter', 'azure-cqrs']],
      ['An internal admin tool has 12 tables, edit-a-row forms and 50 users, and reads roughly equal writes. An architect proposes CQRS with an event-sourced write side and three projections. Your view?', ['Keep one model; a simple CRUD tool gains nothing and pays for sync and lag', 'Adopt it, because separate read models let reads and writes scale apart', 'Adopt it, because 50 users editing the same rows will cause lock contention', 'Use read replicas instead, so that reads do not compete with the writes'], 'CQRS earns its cost when reads and writes have very different shapes or loads, or the domain is complex. Here there is little load and no asymmetry, so the result would be a synchronisation pipeline, eventual consistency and more code. Fowler warns that for most systems CQRS adds risky complexity.', ['fowler-cqrs']],
      ['A team says "we already do CQRS: reads go to two Postgres replicas". Product search now needs full-text ranking and facets that the relational schema serves badly. What would a CQRS read model add?', ['A store with its own shape for that query, rebuildable from the source', 'Fresher results, since projections apply changes sooner than replicas do', 'More write capacity, as writes are split between the two models', 'Strong consistency, as each query reads from a store of its own'], 'A replica is the same schema in the same engine, so it scales the queries the schema already serves well. A read model can be a search index shaped for ranking and facets, and can be rebuilt from the source when its design changes. It is still eventually consistent, and every write still goes to the write model.', ['azure-cqrs', 'azure-materialized-view']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain CQRS: what is separated, how the read side is kept up to date, and when you would not use it.', ['A write model for commands and business rules; read models shaped for queries', 'Projections fed by events or CDC, with an outbox rather than a dual write, and idempotent consumers', 'Read models lag; read-your-writes by reading the write side or waiting for a sequence number', 'How it differs from read replicas and from event sourcing', 'Not worth it for simple CRUD domains']],
    duck: ['Why not keep one table and add indexes for the slow queries?', 'If the read side is behind, how do I know my change went through?'],
    lab: { type: 'sort', brief: 'Where does each piece belong in a CQRS system?', buckets: ['Write model (commands)', 'Read model (queries)', 'Keeping them in sync'], items: [
      ['Reject "book room" when the room is already taken', 0], ['A denormalised order-history document for each customer', 1], ['An outbox row written in the same transaction as the order', 2],
      ['A search index with facets for browsing products', 1], ['An optimistic concurrency check on the aggregate\'s version', 0], ['A CDC connector reading the database\'s log', 2],
      ['Normalised tables guarded by foreign keys', 0], ['A projector that skips events it has already applied', 2], ['A view dropped and rebuilt after its schema changes', 1],
      ['Checking that an order stays under the credit limit', 0], ['A leaderboard kept as a sorted set in Redis', 1], ['An alert when a projection falls 30 s behind', 2]
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '3.6', mod: '3', title: 'Saga pattern', tag: 'Local commits, undone by compensation',
    analogy: 'Booking a trip with a flight, a hotel and a car from three firms. None of them will hold a booking provisional while you wait for the others, so you book each in turn; if the car falls through, you cancel the hotel and then the flight, and pay any cancellation fee. Cancelling is a new action, not the booking un-happening.',
    points: [
      'Two-phase commit across services needs a coordinator: participants prepare, then wait for its decision while still holding their locks. A coordinator outage leaves them blocked, and the database or the message broker may not support 2PC at all. With a database per service, most designs avoid it.',
      'A saga (Garcia-Molina and Salem, 1987) is a sequence of local transactions T1…Tn, each committed on its own, with a compensating transaction Ci for each. If Tk fails, Ck−1…C1 run. A compensation undoes the step\'s meaning ("release 2 seats"), not its bytes, because other transactions may have changed the data since.',
      'Order the steps around the pivot, the point of no return: compensable steps first, then the pivot, then retryable steps that are idempotent and retried until they succeed. Put irreversible actions after every validation that could still fail.',
      'Choreography: each service reacts to the previous one\'s events, with no central controller; fine for a few steps, hard to follow as it grows, with a risk of cyclic dependencies. Orchestration: an orchestrator tells each participant what to do and records the saga\'s state, which suits longer flows but makes the orchestrator a component that must not lose that state.',
      'There is no isolation between sagas: others see intermediate states, giving lost updates and dirty reads. Countermeasures include semantic locks (an order in a PENDING state that other requests must respect), commutative updates, and rereading values before acting.',
      'Every step and every compensation is retried after crashes, so each must be idempotent. Each step must also commit its change and its outgoing message atomically, which is the transactional outbox; the relay can publish twice, so consumers deduplicate. Compensations can fail too: record progress, retry, and alert a human when retries run out.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Writing a compensation as "put back the old values". Between the step and its undo, other transactions changed the same rows, so the restore overwrites their work. Compensate with the inverse business action, such as releasing exactly the seats this saga reserved.',
    say: 'Checkout is an orchestrated saga: create the order as PENDING, reserve stock, then charge the card as the pivot; shipping comes after and is only ever retried. If the charge is declined, the orchestrator releases the stock and rejects the order. Every step writes an outbox row, and every handler deduplicates on the message ID.',
    refs: [
      ['helland-life-beyond-dt', '2PC blocks — “This includes 2PC (two phase commit) which can easily block when nodes are unavailable and other protocols which do not block in the face of node failures such as the Paxos algorithm.”'],
      ['pg-prepare-transaction', 'A prepared transaction keeps its locks — “Keep in mind also that the transaction continues to hold whatever locks it held.”'],
      ['sagas-1987', 'Definition — “A LLT is a saga if it can be written as a sequence of transactions that can be interleaved with other transactions”'],
      ['sagas-1987', 'Semantic undo — “The compensating transaction undoes, from a semantic point of view, any of the actions performed by Ti, but does not necessarily return the database to the state that existed when the execution of Ti began”'],
      ['azure-saga', 'The pivot — “Pivot transactions serve as the point of no return in the saga. After a pivot transaction succeeds, compensable transactions are no longer relevant.”'],
      ['azure-saga', 'Orchestration — “In orchestration, a centralized controller, or orchestrator, handles all the transactions and tells the participants which operation to perform based on events.”'],
      ['azure-saga', 'Semantic lock — “Semantic lock: Use application-level locks when a saga\'s compensable transaction uses a semaphore to indicate that an update is in progress.”'],
      ['azure-compensating-transaction', 'Compensations can fail — “Compensating transactions are eventually consistent operations and can fail. The system should record progress so that it can resume the compensating transaction from the point of failure.”'],
      ['aws-saga-orchestration', 'Idempotent participants — “Saga participants need to be idempotent to allow repeated execution in case of transient failures caused by unexpected crashes and orchestrator failures.”'],
      ['microservices-io-transactional-outbox', 'Not every store can join — “The database and/or the message broker might not support 2PC.”'],
      ['aws-sqs-fifo-key-terms', 'Body-based dedup, 5-minute window — “If multiple messages with the same deduplication ID are sent within a 5 minute deduplication interval, they are treated as duplicates, and only one copy is delivered. If you don\'t specify a deduplication ID and content-based deduplication is enabled, Amazon SQS generates a deduplication ID by hashing the message body.”'],
      ['microservices-io-transactional-outbox', 'The relay can repeat — “The Message relay might publish a message more than once.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['Why do services with their own databases avoid two-phase commit?', 'Prepared participants hold their locks until the coordinator decides, so a coordinator failure leaves them all blocked, and even one slow participant holds everyone\'s locks until the coordinator gives up on it. The database or the message broker may not support 2PC at all, and the protocol couples every service\'s availability to every other\'s.'],
      ['Choreography or orchestration: how do you choose?', 'Choreography (services react to each other\'s events) suits a few steps and has no central component. Orchestration (one controller issues commands and records state) suits longer or changing flows and makes the flow visible in one place, at the cost of a component that must be durable.'],
      ['What is the pivot transaction, and what happens if a step after it fails?', 'The point of no return: once it commits, the saga must complete. Steps after it are retryable and idempotent, so a failure there is retried until it succeeds; earlier steps are not compensated.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['An orchestrated saga calls 5 services in turn; each step costs 40 ms of work plus 10 ms of messaging. Step 5\'s local transaction fails and rolls back at the end of its 50 ms. Compensations cost 50 ms each, run in sequence, and the client is told the order is rejected when the last one (undoing step 1, which created the order) completes. When is that?', ['After 450 ms', 'After 250 ms', 'After 500 ms', 'After 400 ms'], 'Forward: 5 × 50 = 250 ms. Step 5 rolled back its own local transaction, so only steps 4, 3, 2 and 1 are compensated: 4 × 50 = 200 ms. Total 450 ms. 250 ms is when step 5 fails, before any compensation; 500 ms also compensates the step that failed; 400 ms leaves out the messaging cost on the forward steps.', 'calc'],
      ['A saga approves an order in its last step. Midway through, a cancel request for the same order arrives and its handler releases the order\'s stock; the saga then approves an order with no stock. Which saga countermeasure prevents this without a distributed transaction?', ['A PENDING order state that other requests must respect until the saga ends', 'Checking the stock level once, at the saga\'s start, before any step runs', 'Retrying the approval step until it succeeds, as approval comes after the pivot', 'More saga workers, so that the window between the steps gets shorter'], 'Sagas have no isolation, so other requests see intermediate state. A semantic lock marks the order as in progress, and the cancel handler must wait, queue or refuse. A stock check at the start cannot see a release that happens midway; a shorter window still lets the race happen; and retrying the approval does not bring the stock back.', ['azure-saga']],
      ['A trip saga books a refundable hotel, buys a non-refundable flight ticket, and awards loyalty points, a step that can be retried safely but cannot be withdrawn once the customer has them. In what order should the steps run?', ['Hotel, flight, points: nothing after the flight needs undoing', 'Flight first, so the hardest step fails before anything else', 'Loyalty points first, since that step is the least likely to fail', 'Hotel, points, then the flight, so that the riskiest step runs last'], 'The ticket cannot be undone, so it is the pivot. Compensable steps go before it and retryable steps after it, so nothing after the pivot ever needs undoing. Buying the ticket first means a later hotel failure leaves a ticket that cannot be refunded; awarding the points before the flight means a failed purchase leaves points that cannot be withdrawn.', ['azure-saga', 'azure-compensating-transaction']],
      ['Inventory and payments each run PostgreSQL. A 2PC coordinator crashes after both have run PREPARE TRANSACTION and before it sends a decision. What happens to the stock rows the inventory transaction updated?', ['They stay locked until someone runs COMMIT or ROLLBACK PREPARED', 'They are freed when each database aborts after its lock timeout', 'They are committed, since both participants voted yes to prepare', 'They are rolled back once the coordinator\'s connection is dropped'], 'A prepared transaction is stored on disk, detached from the session, and keeps holding its locks until it is resolved, even across restarts. A participant cannot decide alone: having voted yes, it must wait for the decision. This blocking is why 2PC is avoided across services.', ['pg-prepare-transaction', 'helland-life-beyond-dt']],
      ['The payment service\'s outbox relay publishes CardCharged, then crashes before marking the row as sent. It restarts 20 minutes later and publishes CardCharged again. What should the order service do?', ['Ignore the repeat, for example by recording processed message IDs', 'Refund the second charge as a compensation, then continue the saga', 'Nothing special, as the broker drops a message with a repeated body', 'Fail the saga, as a repeated event means the outbox is corrupted'], 'The card was charged once; the event was delivered twice. A relay that crashes between publishing and recording will repeat itself, so consumers must be idempotent, typically by storing the IDs of messages they have handled. Refunding would undo a real payment. Most brokers do not deduplicate by body, and where one does (SQS FIFO content-based deduplication) it covers only a 5-minute window, far shorter than this restart, so the consumer must still deduplicate.', ['microservices-io-transactional-outbox', 'aws-saga-orchestration']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how you would keep an order, its stock reservation and its payment consistent across three services without a distributed transaction.', ['Why not 2PC: prepared participants hold locks and block on the coordinator; some stores cannot join', 'A saga: local transactions with semantic compensations, run backwards on failure', 'Steps ordered around a pivot: compensable, pivot, retryable', 'Choreography vs orchestration, and when each fits', 'No isolation (semantic locks); idempotent steps; an outbox for state plus message']],
    duck: ['If a later step fails, why can\'t the database just roll everything back?', 'What happens if the undo step fails as well?'],
    lab: { type: 'order', brief: 'An orchestrated checkout saga runs: create order, reserve stock, charge card. The card is declined, and completed steps are compensated in reverse order. Put the events in order.', items: [
      'The client POSTs /orders and the orchestrator starts the saga',
      'Order service creates the order in state PENDING',
      'Inventory service reserves the items',
      'Payment service tries to charge the card, and it is declined',
      'Inventory service releases the reservation (compensation)',
      'Order service marks the order REJECTED (compensation)',
      'The client polls GET /orders/{id} and sees REJECTED'
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '3.7', mod: '3', title: 'Batch processing', tag: 'Whole-dataset jobs you can safely re-run',
    analogy: 'Counting an election. Every polling station counts its own ballots and sends a tally per candidate to the regional count, which adds up each candidate\'s tallies. If one station\'s tally is lost, its sealed ballot box is recounted; nobody recounts the country.',
    points: [
      'MapReduce: map turns each input record into key/value pairs; the framework groups the pairs by key, routing each key to one of R reduce tasks (by default hash(key) mod R); reduce combines all the values for a key. Google reported in 2004 that it often ran jobs of 200,000 map tasks and 5,000 reduce tasks on 2,000 machines.',
      'Fault tolerance is re-execution. Tasks are deterministic functions of immutable input, so a dead worker\'s tasks simply run again elsewhere, including map tasks it had finished, because their output sat on its local disk. Output files are committed by atomic rename, so a task that ran twice is counted once.',
      'The shuffle that moves data from map to reduce is the costly step (disk, serialisation, network), and it is where skew bites: every record for a hot key lands in one task, which runs long after the rest. Remedies: pre-aggregate on the map side (MapReduce\'s combiner) so fewer records cross the network, or let Spark\'s adaptive execution (on by default since Spark 3.2) split sort-merge join partitions over 5× the median and over 256 MB (the defaults).',
      'Spark keeps intermediate data in memory between steps instead of writing it out after every job, and recovers by lineage: it records the transformations that built each partition, and recomputes only the lost partitions from their parents rather than keeping replicas.',
      'Lambda architecture: the same immutable input feeds a batch system and a stream system in parallel, and queries stitch their results together. Its cost is the same logic written and run in two systems. Kappa (Kreps, 2014): one streaming job; to reprocess, run a second copy from the start of the retained log into a new table and switch over.',
      'Code changes and bugs mean history is recomputed. Make each run replace exactly the partitions it computes, so a rerun or a backfill gives the same table. In Spark, dynamic partitionOverwriteMode replaces only the partitions written; the default static mode first deletes every partition that matches the statement\'s partition specification (for data source tables; Hive serde tables are always overwritten in dynamic mode).'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Recomputing one week of a Spark data source table with INSERT OVERWRITE in the default static mode and no fixed partition value. Every partition matches the empty specification, so all of them are deleted, and only the recomputed week comes back.',
    say: 'The nightly job reads immutable raw events and overwrites one date partition per run, so a rerun, or a 90-day backfill after a bug fix, is the same job over past dates. For fresh numbers I would replay a retained log through one streaming job rather than keep the same logic in a batch and a speed layer.',
    refs: [
      ['mapreduce-osdi04', 'Routing keys to reducers — “Reduce invocations are distributed by partitioning the intermediate key space into R pieces using a partitioning function (e.g., hash(key) mod R).”'],
      ['mapreduce-osdi04', 'Finished map work is redone — “Completed map tasks are re-executed on a failure because their output is stored on the local disk(s) of the failed machine and is therefore inaccessible.”'],
      ['mapreduce-osdi04', 'Why re-execution is safe — “When the user-supplied map and reduce operators are deterministic functions of their input values, our distributed implementation produces the same output as would have been produced by a non-faulting sequential execution of the entire program.”'],
      ['spark-rdd-guide', 'The shuffle is expensive — “The Shuffle is an expensive operation since it involves disk I/O, data serialization, and network I/O.”'],
      ['spark-sql-performance', 'AQE is on by default — “Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) is an optimization technique in Spark SQL that makes use of the runtime statistics to choose the most efficient query execution plan, which is enabled by default since Apache Spark 3.2.0.”'],
      ['spark-sql-performance', 'Skew detection (defaults 5.0 and 256MB) — “A partition is considered as skewed if its size is larger than this factor multiplying the median partition size and also larger than spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.skewedPartitionThresholdInBytes.”'],
      ['spark-rdd-guide', 'Default storage level — “The cache() method is a shorthand for using the default storage level, which is StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY (store deserialized objects in memory).”'],
      ['spark-rdd-guide', 'groupByKey for sums — “Note: If you are grouping in order to perform an aggregation (such as a sum or average) over each key, using reduceByKey or aggregateByKey will yield much better performance.”'],
      ['aws-emr-data-skew', 'Salting — “By adding a random value or "salt" to a skewed column, such as \'col1\', we aim to distribute the data more evenly among processing units.”'],
      ['spark-rdd-nsdi12', 'Lineage instead of replicas — “This allows them to efficiently provide fault tolerance by logging the transformations used to build a dataset (its lineage) rather than the actual data.”'],
      ['spark-rdd-nsdi12', 'Only lost partitions are recomputed — “Furthermore, only the lost partitions of an RDD need to be recomputed upon failure, and they can be recomputed in parallel on different nodes, without having to roll back the whole program.”'],
      ['kreps-questioning-lambda', 'Lambda means two code paths — “You implement your transformation logic twice, once in the batch system and once in the stream processing system.”'],
      ['kreps-questioning-lambda', 'Reprocessing with one job — “When you want to do the reprocessing, start a second instance of your stream processing job that starts processing from the beginning of the retained data, but direct this output data to a new output table.”'],
      ['spark-configuration', 'Static vs dynamic overwrite — “In static mode, Spark deletes all the partitions that match the partition specification(e.g. PARTITION(a=1,b)) in the INSERT statement, before overwriting. In dynamic mode, Spark doesn\'t delete partitions ahead, and only overwrite those partitions that have data written into it at runtime.”'],
      ['spark-configuration', 'Hive serde tables — “Note that this config doesn\'t affect Hive serde tables, as they are always overwritten with dynamic mode.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['Why can MapReduce recover from a failed worker just by re-running its tasks?', 'Tasks are deterministic functions of immutable input, so running one again gives the same output. Outputs are committed atomically (rename), so a duplicate run leaves one result. Finished map tasks on the dead machine are re-run too, because their output lived on its local disk.'],
      ['What is skew in a shuffle, and how do you deal with it?', 'One key (or a few) carries a large share of the records; hashing sends all of them to one task, which runs long after the others. Pre-aggregate on the map side (a combiner), salt the hot key and aggregate in two stages (for mergeable aggregates such as sums), or let Spark AQE split skewed join partitions.'],
      ['Lambda vs Kappa?', 'Lambda: a batch layer and a speed layer compute the same views in two systems, merged at query time; every change is written and debugged twice. Kappa: one streaming job over a retained log; reprocessing is a second copy of the job replaying from the start into a new table.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A job groups 2 billion events by advertiser over 1,000 reduce tasks, hash-partitioned by advertiser. One advertiser has 10% of all events. Each task processes 1M records a minute. Roughly how long do the tasks take?', ['~200 min for the hot task; ~2 min for the others', '~2 min per task, since hashing evens out the load', '~2,000 min, since one task reads the full 2B records', '~0.2 min, since the hot key is split across 1,000 tasks'], 'All 200M records for the hot key hash to one task: 200 minutes. The other 1.8B spread over 999 tasks, about 1.8M each: about 2 minutes. Hashing spreads keys, not the records within a key; 2,000 minutes would mean every record went to one task.', 'calc'],
      ['Halfway through a MapReduce job, a worker dies. It had finished 40 map tasks and was running 2 reduce tasks. What does the master schedule again?', ['The 40 finished map tasks and the 2 reduce tasks', 'Just the 2 reduce tasks; finished map output is kept', 'The whole job, because intermediate data is now partial', 'Nothing; copies of its map output exist on other nodes'], 'Map output is written to the worker\'s local disk, not to the replicated file system, so it died with the machine and must be recomputed. Completed reduce output is already in the global file system and is safe. Deterministic tasks make re-running them harmless.', ['mapreduce-osdi04']],
      ['A team runs a nightly Spark job and a Flink job that compute the same revenue per merchant; the numbers differ by 0.5% and every logic change ships twice. Dashboards need minute-level freshness and Kafka keeps 30 days. What does Kreps propose?', ['One streaming job; reprocess by replaying the log into a new table', 'A shared library that compiles the same logic for both engines', 'Drop the streaming job and serve the nightly batch views alone', 'Run the batch job hourly so that the speed layer can be removed'], 'Kreps\'s alternative to Lambda is a single stream processor: reprocessing is a second instance of the same job reading from the start of the retained log into a new output table, then switching readers over. He argues an abstraction over both engines still leaves two systems to run and debug; nightly or hourly batch misses the freshness need.', ['kreps-questioning-lambda']],
      ['A Spark job has a 2 TB RDD cached in memory with cache(), across 100 executors. One executor dies, taking 20 GB of cached partitions with it. What does Spark do?', ['Recomputes only the lost partitions from their lineage', 'Restores the lost partitions from replicas on other nodes', 'Restarts the job from its input, as memory is not durable', 'Fails the job, as the cached data was never checkpointed'], 'Spark records how each partition was derived, so it recomputes just the missing partitions, in parallel, from their parents. The default level for RDD cache(), MEMORY_ONLY, keeps no replicas (only the replicated levels such as MEMORY_ONLY_2 do), and recovery needs neither a checkpoint nor a whole-job restart.', ['spark-rdd-nsdi12', 'spark-rdd-guide']],
      ['A bug inflated revenue for 1–14 March. The fix reruns the job with df.write.mode("overwrite").insertInto("revenue") on a DataFrame holding only those 14 days; revenue is a Spark data source table (USING parquet), partitioned by date, holding two years. partitionOverwriteMode is left at its default. Result?', ['Every partition is deleted first; only the 14 recomputed days remain', 'Only the 14 March partitions are replaced; all others are left alone', 'The new rows are appended, so 1–14 March now appear twice', 'Spark refuses, as an overwrite needs a fixed partition value'], 'The default is static mode, which deletes every partition matching the INSERT\'s partition specification before writing; with no fixed value, that is all of them. Replacing only the 14 written partitions is dynamic mode, which has to be switched on. This is why backfills set dynamic mode or name the partition explicitly.', ['spark-configuration']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how a large batch job survives machine failures, where it gets slow, and how you would reprocess history after a bug fix.', ['Map, shuffle by key, reduce; deterministic tasks over immutable input', 'Failures handled by re-running tasks (MapReduce) or recomputing lost partitions from lineage (Spark)', 'The shuffle is the expensive step; skew makes one task the job\'s tail; salting, combiners, AQE', 'Lambda keeps two code paths; Kappa replays a retained log through one job', 'Backfills overwrite exactly their own partitions, so reruns are idempotent']],
    duck: ['If a machine dies halfway through, why not just restart the whole job?', 'Why would one task take hours when the other thousand take minutes?'],
    lab: { type: 'pick', want: 3, brief: 'A nightly Spark 3.1 job (AQE off) joins raw events to the customer table on customer_id, then totals each customer\'s amounts with RDD groupByKey followed by a sum. It now takes 9 hours. In both the join stage and the aggregation stage, one task holding a single huge customer runs for hours while 999 finish in minutes. Pick the three best responses.', options: [
      ['Salt the hot customer_id and sum in two stages', true, 'Spreads one key\'s records over many tasks, then adds up the partial sums.'],
      ['Turn on AQE skew-join handling for the join stage', true, 'Spark splits the oversized join partition into evenly sized tasks; AQE is on by default only from Spark 3.2.'],
      ['Replace groupByKey with reduceByKey for the totals', true, 'Values combine on each mapper, so one partial sum per mapper moves, not every record.'],
      ['Double the number of executors in the cluster', false, 'The 999 fast tasks were never the problem; one task still holds the hot key.'],
      ['Raise shuffle partitions from 1,000 to 4,000', false, 'More partitions spread more keys, but one key still hashes to one partition.'],
      ['Turn on speculative execution for slow tasks', false, 'A backup copy of the slow task gets the same records and is just as slow.'],
      ['Give each executor twice as much memory', false, 'May reduce spilling, but the one task still has to process every hot record.'],
      ['Move the job to run an hour earlier each night', false, 'Changes when it finishes, not how long the skewed task takes.']
    ] }
  });
})(SD.T);
